PREFIX app: <did:ng:z:cairns/>
PREFIX schema: <https://schema.org/>
PREFIX xsd: <http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#>
PREFIX geo: <http://www.w3.org/2003/01/geo/wgs84_pos#>
PREFIX owl: <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#>

# Places, wherever they live (Specs §3.2, §5). One shape covers both kinds a
# memory can point at, because the difference is in the IRI, not in the data:
#
#   - an identified place has its own document and its own URI, which may have
#     been written by this app, by another app, or synced in from elsewhere;
#   - an unnamed location is a subject inside the memory's own document, so its
#     IRI is `<memory doc>#place-N`. Not referenceable, not indexed, and never
#     offered by the picker — that is the trade §3.2 makes for not minting
#     identity.
#
# Everything is optional but the type: a gazetteer entry may have no
# coordinates, an unnamed location no name, and a half-synced document neither
# (§8, "partially loaded"). A place with nothing on it renders as its
# coordinates or as nothing at all, which beats not resolving it.
#
# Coordinates are written twice: as §3.2's `schema:geo`, which is what another
# application reads, and as a flat WGS84 pair on the place itself, which is
# what this one can read. Spike 7 showed a nested object written by SPARQL is
# invisible to the ORM, so the `schema:geo` node — a `schema:GeoCoordinates` one
# level down — cannot be read back through this shape today.
#
# The flat pair is therefore TEMPORARY: it is a workaround for a NextGraph
# limitation, not a modelling choice, and it goes when the ORM can resolve a
# nested object it did not write itself. `schema:geo` is the durable form and
# both are always written together, so nothing has to be migrated when it does
# — the reader just changes which one it looks at.
app:PlaceShape EXTRA a {
  a [schema:Place] ;
  schema:name xsd:string ? ;
  # Declared so the shape describes what the app writes; unresolvable for now
  # (it arrives as a bare IRI), which is exactly the limitation above.
  schema:geo IRI ? ;
  geo:lat xsd:decimal ? ;
  geo:long xsd:decimal ? ;
  schema:address xsd:string ? ;
  # The containing chain, for the transitive place facet of the S-22 filter.
  schema:containedInPlace IRI ? ;
  # Set when a local place was reconciled with an external one (S-33).
  owl:sameAs IRI ? ;
}
